XwaylandWindow: heal window state on destroy

When an Xwayland surface is destroyed without a preceding unmap (the X
client crashing, override-redirect tear-down, gamescope exiting), the
listener sets `Window.impl = .destroying` while leaving `Window.state`
at `.ready`, `.initialized`, or `.mapped`. The next
`WindowManager.renderFinish` loop then calls `Window.destroy`, which
asserts `unreachable` for those states.

Reproduced reliably by launching Steam under gamescope nested inside
river: gamescope's eventual exit triggered the crash on every attempt.

`XdgToplevel.handleDestroy` already heals this case for XDG toplevels.
Mirror that logic here. Unlike XDG, X11 does not guarantee
unmap-before-destroy, so `.mapped` must be handled gracefully rather
than asserted unreachable.

diff --git a/river/XwaylandWindow.zig b/river/XwaylandWindow.zig
index 7d16d97..6ba7e63 100644
--- a/river/XwaylandWindow.zig
+++ b/river/XwaylandWindow.zig
@@ -167,6 +167,18 @@ fn handleDestroy(listener: *wl.Listener(void)) void {

     const window = xwindow.window;
     window.impl = .destroying;
+    // X11 does not guarantee unmap-before-destroy the way XDG does, so the
+    // window may still be in .ready / .initialized / .mapped here. Mirror
+    // XdgToplevel.handleDestroy() and route through .closing so the next
+    // manage cycle drives Window.destroy() down its valid path instead of
+    // hitting the unreachable arm.
+    switch (window.state) {
+        .init, .closing => {},
+        .ready, .initialized, .mapped => {
+            window.state = .closing;
+            server.wm.dirtyWindowing();
+        },
+    }
 }

